    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(77, 41), (24, 54), (82, 42), (82, 40), (20, 45)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 16.0, 18.0, 41.0]\n    generator_capacities = [62.0, 81.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 240  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 110  # kV\n    line_capacity = 150  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 82097  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 40  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning approach for the five\u2011node transmission network\n\nWe have five electrical nodes distributed across our service area. Their map locations are provided in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (77, 41)\n  - N1 (generator): (24, 54)\n  - N2 (consumer): (82, 42)\n  - N3 (consumer): (82, 40)\n  - N4 (consumer): (20, 45), and the functional roles of each (two generators and three consumers) are indicated in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. Our task is to determine which transmission lines to build between these nodes so that power can be delivered reliably and cost\u2011effectively.\n\nOn the demand side, each consumer must be supplied with sufficient power to meet the requirements listed in Consumer demands: N2: 16.0 MW, N3: 18.0 MW, N4: 41.0 MW. On the supply side, each generator has a maximum output defined in Generator capacities: N0: 62.0 MW, N1: 81.0 MW, and no line we build may be scheduled above its thermal rating, which is specified by 150 MW. Power can only flow along lines we choose to construct; at the nodes themselves, we will assume lossless conservation of flow for planning purposes. In other words, the nodal balances will not deduct physical losses from the power delivered; instead, we will account for those losses as a separate operating cost.\n\nThere are two cost components to weigh:\n\n1) Construction cost. New transmission lines incur capital expense that scales with route length. Using the geometry implied by Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (77, 41)\n  - N1 (generator): (24, 54)\n  - N2 (consumer): (82, 42)\n  - N3 (consumer): (82, 40)\n  - N4 (consumer): (20, 45), each candidate connection will have a distance\u2011based build cost at the rate in $82,097/km. Shorter routes reduce capital, but they may not be optimal when we consider operating costs.\n\n2) Operating cost from resistive losses. We will use copper conductors, with the material specification given in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper) and the conductor size given in 240 mm\u00b2. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with cross\u2011sectional area. The corresponding heating losses follow a square law with respect to power flow: doubling the scheduled power on a line drives four times the resistive losses. This is the key physics we must capture; linear approximations would misstate the true cost drivers. The loss cost will be computed from the physical relationship that loss scales as resistance times the square of power over voltage (Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power / Voltage)^2), and we will apply the financial penalty in $40/MWh to that lost energy. The selected operating voltage, 110 kV, directly influences these losses: higher voltage lowers current for a given power, and because losses depend on the square of current, the cost has an inverse\u2011square sensitivity to voltage.\n\nPutting this together, the planning problem is to choose which lines to build and how to route power from the generators to the consumers to meet all demands within generator and line limits, while minimizing the sum of construction cost and the quadratic operating \u201cheating\u201d cost. Because resistive losses grow with the square of flow, the network topology we select should avoid long, high\u2011flow corridors where possible, or leverage higher voltage to mitigate those losses. The model will conserve power at nodes and treat losses strictly as a financial penalty, not a physical deduction from delivered energy.\n\nWe will proceed by evaluating candidate line routings between the five nodes, using distance\u2011based capital costs and physics\u2011based quadratic loss costs. The recommended design will be the configuration that meets all demands and capacity constraints at minimum total cost, reflecting both the upfront build expense and the ongoing cost of resistive heating.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 Five-node transmission network design with quadratic loss costs\n\nOur utility is preparing to build a transmission network that connects two generator sites to three consumer locations across five nodes. The goal is to meet all consumer demands reliably while minimizing the combined cost of line construction and power losses due to resistive heating.\n\nWe have five nodes positioned at map coordinates in {NODE_COORDINATES}, with their roles (generators vs. consumers) defined in {NODE_TYPES}. Consumer requirements are provided in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and generator output limits are listed in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}. The planning decision is to determine which transmission lines to build between node pairs and how to route power across the resulting network. Power can only flow along lines that we choose to construct, and any built line must operate within a maximum throughput of {LINE_CAPACITY}. The system will run at the voltage specified in {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}.\n\nConstruction costs are distance-driven. For each candidate corridor we evaluate, we will compute route length from the map coordinates and apply the per-kilometer build cost in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. Longer routes will increase capital outlay and also tend to increase operational losses, so distance matters in both parts of the objective.\n\nOperational costs arise from resistive losses in the conductors. We will use copper, with the material specification given in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY} and the selected conductor size defined in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Physically, line resistance increases with the length of the run and decreases with thicker conductors. This means long, thin lines are more expensive to operate, while shorter or larger cross-section lines reduce losses.\n\nThese losses obey a square law with current (I\u00b2R). In practical terms, if we double the power flow on a line, the resistive heating increases by roughly four times. Because current equals power divided by voltage, operating at higher voltage reduces current and mitigates this effect; the loss cost on a line is governed by the physical relationship Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)\u00b2, and therefore falls with the square of the voltage (a 1/V\u00b2 factor).\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat losses as a financial penalty rather than a physical subtraction from delivered energy. Power is conserved at the nodes: consumers must receive their full demand, and generators cannot exceed their limits. We then pay an external operating cost for the heating on each built line, priced at {LOSS_COST_RATE} based on the physics-based quadratic loss. This approach avoids misleading linear approximations; it correctly captures that high flows on a given segment become disproportionately expensive.\n\nIn summary, the objective is to minimize total cost: the sum of distance-based construction spending and the quadratic operational cost of resistive losses. The optimal design will strike a balance between:\n- Building new lines (and potentially parallel paths) to spread flows and avoid high currents on any single segment.\n- Choosing routes that are short enough to limit both capital cost and resistance.\n- Leveraging the operating voltage to reduce the I\u00b2R penalty.\n\nDeliverables will include the recommended set of lines to build among the five nodes, expected power flows on each built segment, and a transparent accounting of total cost under this square-law loss model. This ensures our investment plan reflects the real physics of transmission, not a simplified linear proxy, and meets all demands with generators and lines operating within their limits."
}